{"id":"policy-resource-quotas","corpus":"k8s","title":"Resource Quotas","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/policy/resource-quotas/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Resource Quotas"},{"kind":"p","text":"When several users or teams share a cluster with a fixed number of nodes, there is a concern that one team could use more than its fair share of resources."},{"kind":"p","text":"Resource quotas are a tool for administrators to address this concern."},{"kind":"p","text":"A resource quota, defined by a ResourceQuota object, provides constraints that limit aggregate resource consumption per namespace. A ResourceQuota can also limit the quantity of objects that can be created in a namespace by API kind, as well as the total amount of infrastructure resources that may be consumed by API objects found in that namespace."},{"kind":"p","text":"Neither contention nor changes to quota will affect already created resources."},{"kind":"h2","text":"How Kubernetes ResourceQuotas work"},{"kind":"p","text":"ResourceQuotas work like this:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Different teams work in different namespaces. This separation can be enforced with RBAC or any other authorization mechanism."},{"kind":"li","text":"A cluster administrator creates at least one ResourceQuota for each namespace."},{"kind":"li","text":"To make sure the enforcement stays enforced, the cluster administrator should also restrict access to delete or update that ResourceQuota; for example, by defining a ValidatingAdmissionPolicy."},{"kind":"li","text":"Users create resources (pods, services, etc.) in the namespace, and the quota system tracks usage to ensure it does not exceed hard resource limits defined in a ResourceQuota. You can apply a scope to a ResourceQuota to limit where it applies,"},{"kind":"li","text":"If creating or updating a resource violates a quota constraint, the control plane rejects that request with HTTP status code 403 Forbidden. The error includes a message explaining the constraint that would have been violated."},{"kind":"li","text":"If quotas are enabled in a namespace for resource such as cpu and memory, users must specify requests or limits for those values when they define a Pod; otherwise, the quota system may reject pod creation. The resource quota walkthrough shows an example of how to avoid this problem."},{"kind":"li","text":"You can define a LimitRange to force defaults on pods that make no compute resource requirements (so that users don't have to remember to do that)."},{"kind":"p","text":"You often do not create Pods directly; for example, you more usually create a workload management object such as a Deployment. If you create a Deployment that tries to use more resources than are available, the creation of the Deployment (or other workload management object) succeeds, but the Deployment may not be able to get all of the Pods it manages to exist. In that case you can check the status of the Deployment, for example with kubectl describe, to see what has happened."},{"kind":"li","text":"For cpu and memory resources, ResourceQuotas enforce that every (new) pod in that namespace sets a limit for that resource. If you enforce a resource quota in a namespace for either cpu or memory, you and other clients, must specify either requests or limits for that resource, for every new Pod you submit. If you don't, the control plane may reject admission for that Pod."},{"kind":"li","text":"For other resources: ResourceQuota works and will ignore pods in the namespace without setting a limit or request for that resource. It means that you can create a new pod without limit/request for ephemeral storage if the resource quota limits the ephemeral storage of this namespace."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can use a LimitRange to automatically set a default request for these resources."},{"kind":"p","text":"The name of a ResourceQuota object must be a valid DNS subdomain name."},{"kind":"p","text":"Examples of policies that could be created using namespaces and quotas are:"},{"kind":"li","text":"In a cluster with a capacity of 32 GiB RAM, and 16 cores, let team A use 20 GiB and 10 cores, let B use 10GiB and 4 cores, and hold 2GiB and 2 cores in reserve for future allocation."},{"kind":"li","text":"Limit the \"testing\" namespace to using 1 core and 1GiB RAM. Let the \"production\" namespace use any amount."},{"kind":"p","text":"In the case where the total capacity of the cluster is less than the sum of the quotas of the namespaces, there may be contention for resources. This is handled on a first-come-first-served basis."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Enabling Resource Quota"},{"kind":"p","text":"ResourceQuota support is enabled by default for many Kubernetes distributions. It is enabled when the API server --enable-admission-plugins= flag has ResourceQuota as one of its arguments."},{"kind":"p","text":"A resource quota is enforced in a particular namespace when there is a ResourceQuota in that namespace."}],"lemmas":{"quotas":["quota","quotas"],"whens":["whens","when"],"severals":["severals","several"],"users":["user","users"],"teams":["team","teams"],"teamed":["teamed","team"],"teaming":["teaming","team"],"shares":["share","shares"],"shared":["shared","share"],"sharing":["sharing","share"],"clusters":["cluster","clusters"],"clustering":["clustering","cluster"],"clustered":["clustered","cluster"],"fixed":["fixed","fix"],"fixeds":["fixeds","fixed"],"fixes":["fix","fixes"],"fixing":["fixing","fix"],"numbers":["number","numbers"],"numbering":["numbering","number"],"numbered":["numbered","number"],"nodes":["node","nod","nodes"],"nods":["nod","nods"],"nodded":["nodded","nod"],"nodding":["nodding","nod"],"concerns":["concern","concerns"],"concerning":["concerning","concern"],"concerned":["concerned","concern"],"ones":["one","ones"],"uses":["use","uses"],"used":["used","use"],"its":["its",